sion of ${(1-2z)}^{7}$ is" :question/worked-solution "The binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nIn particular, the ${x}^{4}$ term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)(n-3)}{4!}{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-2z$ and $n$ with $7$ in the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7(7-1)(7-2)(7-3)}{4!}\\cdot (-2z)^{4} & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4}{24}\\cdot (-2)^{4}\\cdot {z}^{4} \\\\  & =35\\cdot 16{z}^{4} \\\\  & =560{z}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${z}^{4}$ is $560.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-136020/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df318a39-103a-53a6-99f8-2ccf3601f3ca"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-136020"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-136014/selection/ff106a8cde5593a3e8cd6194234252d8ae8a09eb31ddc0190e1ab01214fb18ae" :answer/id #uuid "44cd0edb-ea68-5221-92f1-bc023303e93f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{91}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-136014/selection/4841ad54878f11d054aabf725790fb4f49bdd53a5ebf6d5bf129c3fe28c1a615" :answer/id #uuid "b875ba8e-8697-5908-bf13-3a6d7bce083e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{91}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-136014/selection/e16546b53c6e5f397b7358c73b72022cb3114afdeb5e1548b7449beaacf2844f" :answer/id #uuid "cf1a1f33-5594-5316-ab0e-997516dc751a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{91}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-136014/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "77090f8a-790b-525f-86dc-2d3fa464dbc4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-136014/selection/37c79160fcb77d334eb933859f5d583ec8bd40b5da33b40ba1cee3d2ce3c629c" :answer/id #uuid "38016097-466a-5256-b8d9-509aee7aafc0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{91}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-136014/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8ead65e7-da07-5368-8ef3-37f1c5106652" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-136014/selection/ff106a8cde5593a3e8cd6194234252d8ae8a09eb31ddc0190e1ab01214fb18ae" :answer-field/choices ["q-136014/selection/ff106a8cde5593a3e8cd6194234252d8ae8a09eb31ddc0190e1ab01214fb18ae" "q-136014/selection/4841ad54878f11d054aabf725790fb4f49bdd53a5ebf6d5bf129c3fe28c1a615" "q-136014/selection/e16546b53c6e5f397b7358c73b72022cb3114afdeb5e1548b7449beaacf2844f" "q-136014/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" "q-136014/selection/37c79160fcb77d334eb933859f5d583ec8bd40b5da33b40ba1cee3d2ce3c629c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-136014" :question/id #uuid "b6ce46c0-0ea2-5270-bc3b-4d0ef451e70d" :question/math-academy-id "q-136014" :question/problem "The coefficient of ${a}^{3}$ in the expansion of ${(1-\\frac{a}{2})}^{14}$ is" :question/worked-solution "The binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nIn particular, the ${x}^{3}$ term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)}{3!}{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-\\frac{a}{2}$ and $n$ with $14$ in the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{14(14-1)(14-2)}{3!}\\cdot {(-\\frac{a}{2})}^{3} & =\\frac{14\\cdot 13\\cdot 12}{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{2}^{3}})\\cdot {a}^{3} \\\\  & =364\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8}){a}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{91}{2}{a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${a}^{3}$ is $-\\frac{91}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-136014/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df318a39-103a-53a6-99f8-2ccf3601f3ca"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-136014"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-251119/field-1/6709fa010e45428208c49c1439a82ee1f143a033c7ade3e967301a9a69909a4d" :answer/id #uuid "32b3da48-9d0a-5324-8a79-e74b5aea5fbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{80}{27}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-251119/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "52a6a249-284d-5b4f-8208-f8521ad70a82" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-251119/field-1/6709fa010e45428208c49c1439a82ee1f143a033c7ade3e967301a9a69909a4d" :answer-field/choices ["q-251119/field-1/6709fa010e45428208c49c1439a82ee1f143a033c7ade3e967301a9a69909a4d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-251119" :question/id #uuid "e65bc0d6-6e4f-55e0-8d41-4057609a4e22" :question/math-academy-id "q-251119" :question/problem "The coefficient of ${u}^{4}$ in the expansion of ${(1+\\frac{2u}{3})}^{6}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The binomial expansion for $(1+x)^{n}$ is given by\n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nIn particular, the ${x}^{4}$ term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)(n-3)}{4!}{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $\\frac{2u}{3}$ and $n$ with $6$ in the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6(6-1)(6-2)(6-3)}{4!}\\cdot {(\\frac{2u}{3})}^{4} & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot 3}{24}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{3})}^{4}\\cdot {u}^{4} \\\\  & =15\\cdot \\frac{16}{81}{u}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{80}{27}{u}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${u}^{4}$ is $\\frac{80}{27}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-251119/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df318a39-103a-53a6-99f8-2ccf3601f3ca"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-251119"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5155"] :question/problem "Find the first $3$ terms in ascending powers of $a$ of the binomial expansion of $(1+3a)^5.$" :question/worked-solution "The first $3$ terms of the binomial expansion for ${(1 + x)^n}$ are given by\n\n$$\n{(1 + x)^n} = 1 + nx + \\frac{{n(n - 1)}}{{2!}}{x^2}  +  \\cdots .\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=3a$ and $n=5$ into the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+3a)}^{5} & =1+5(3a)+\\frac{5(5-1)}{2!}{(3a)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+5(3a)+\\frac{5(4)}{2}(9{a}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & =1+15a+90{a}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9779"] :question/problem "Find the first $3$ terms in ascending powers of $m$ of the binomial expansion of $\\left(1 - \\dfrac{4m}{3}\\right)^7.$" :question/worked-solution "The first $3$ terms of the binomial expansion for ${(1 + x)^n}$ are given by\n\n$$\n{(1 + x)}^n = 1 + nx + \\frac{{n(n - 1)}}{{2!}}{x^2}  +  \\cdots .\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=-\\dfrac{4m}{3}$ and $n=7$ into the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1-\\frac{4m}{3})}^{7} & =1+7(-\\frac{4m}{3})+\\frac{7(7-1)}{2!}{(-\\frac{4m}{3})}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+7(-\\frac{4m}{3})+\\frac{7(6)}{2}{(-\\frac{4m}{3})}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+7(-\\frac{4m}{3})+\\frac{7(6)}{2}(\\frac{16{m}^{2}}{9})+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{28m}{3}+\\frac{112{m}^{2}}{3}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9778"] :question/problem "Determine the coefficient of $r^3$ in the expansion of $\\left( 1 - 3r \\right)^{10}.$" :question/worked-solution "The binomial expansion for $(1 + x)^n$ is given by\n\n$$\n(1 + x)^n = 1 + nx + \\frac{n(n - 1)}{2!} x^2 + \\cdots .\n$$\n\nIn particular, the $x^3$ term is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{n(n-1)(n-2)}{3!} x^3.\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-3r$ and $n$ with $10$ in the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10(10-1)(10-2)}{3!}(-3r)^{3} & =\\frac{10(9)(8)}{6}\\cdot (-3)^{3}\\cdot {r}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{10(9)(8)}{6}\\cdot (-27){r}^{3} \\\\  & =-3\\,240{r}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of $r^3$ is $-3\\,240.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
